    description: 'Doom\'s no-clipping cheat has two versions. In the original 1993 Doom the code is IDSPISPOPD, commonly expanded as "Smashing Pumpkins Into Small Piles Of Putrid Debris"; Doom II (1994) added the simpler IDCLIP. Either way, the cheat turns off the collision checks that keep the player inside the level\'s walls, so the player can walk through solid geometry and explore areas that are normally out of reach.',
    history: 'Doom\'s levels are built in what is often called "2.5D": they look three-dimensional, but the player moves on a flat plane. Walls and floors can be set at any height or angle, but one area cannot sit on top of another, and the engine uses binary space partitioning to work out which surfaces need to be drawn. Clipping is the code that keeps the player inside those walls; noclip simply switches it off, letting the player see how a level\'s spaces fit together from outside.\n\nThe same open structure made Doom easy to modify. Because its level data lived in WAD files separate from the engine, players began building their own maps within months of release, and the Master Levels for Doom II, released in 1995, included more than 3,000 user-created levels. John Romero, one of the game\'s designers, also coined the word "deathmatch" for its competitive multiplayer mode.',
